\fs22 \cf2 The control panel on a Mac with a built-in display or a second screen\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa248\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs30 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Light presets\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa260\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs26 \cf0 A light preset saves brightness, color temperature, and video mode. Choose one from Favorites in the menu or from the panel. Use Mode Center\'85 to save the current light as a preset, or to bind a preset to an app so it's applied when that app comes to the front.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa248\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs30 \cf0 Automation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa260\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs26 \cf0 You can control iScreenBar Menu from AppleScript, Shortcuts, and, on macOS 26, Control Center. Each setting works like its panel button, with the same limits. For example, lamp settings need the iScreenBar connected, and Keep Awake asks for a password.\
AppleScript: in Script Editor, choose File > Open Dictionary and pick iScreenBar Menu. Example: tell application "iScreenBar Menu" to set brightness to 60.\
Shortcuts: search for iScreenBar Menu to find actions such as Set Light, Turn Setting On or Off, Apply Favorite, and Get Lamp Status.\
Control Center: choose Edit Controls and add Lamp Light, Keep Awake, Rotation Preset, Control Panel, Lamp Dimmer, Lamp Brighter, Lamp Warmer, or Lamp Cooler. These are buttons, not switches, and only work while iScreenBar Menu is running.\
Widget: choose Edit Widgets and add Lamp Levels. It shows brightness and color temperature as two bars; click a segment to set that level, or the power button to switch the light. The color temperature bar is unavailable while Auto Temp is on.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa248\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs30 \cf0 Launch at Login and Quit\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa260\partightenfactor0

\fs26 \cf0 Launch at Login
\f1\b0  starts iScreenBar Menu when you log in. It does this with a user LaunchAgent, ~/Library/LaunchAgents/org.windholm.iScreenBarMenu.plist. 
\f0\b Quit
\f1\b0  keeps the app closed until you log in again. It is restarted automatically only if it crashes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa248\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs30 \cf0 Troubleshooting\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs26 \cf0 {\listtext	\'95	}When the app is started at login, it writes its log to ~/Library/Logs/iScreenBarMenu.log.\
{\listtext	\'95	}Don't run the official BenQ app at the same time, because both would change the lamp's settings.\
{\listtext	\'95	}Only the iScreenBar model with USB ID 04A5:2501 has been tested.\
{\listtext	\'95	}Display features use private macOS interfaces, so they may stop working after a macOS update. The app is designed to fail safely and always restore the built-in display.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	\'95	}Run only one copy of the app at a time. Two copies would both control the lamp.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa248\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs30 \cf0 Privacy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa260\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs26 \cf0 iScreenBar Menu doesn't use the network. It talks to the lamp over USB and stores its settings on this Mac only.\
